# Independent REA backup audit

Audited 2026-10-12T10:46:24.957374+00:00.

The main REA Git backup restores successfully from the local bundle without a GitHub fetch. The restored HEAD is `abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71`, matching the saved source checkout. Both the saved mirror and restored repository pass `git fsck --full` with no diagnostics.

- Saved mirror: **1,630 refs** (75 branches, 35 tags, 1,520 pull refs), **5,830 reachable commits**, and **93,357 reachable objects**.
- Bundle: complete history verified, **0 prerequisites**, SHA-256 `5000d05a52cd3fc73c8033e186a8d7267f01a6c770379138afd9ef290dc0f9a1`.
- Restore: identical refs and reachable object IDs; not shallow; no alternates.
- Mirror: not shallow; no partial-clone/promisor configuration, promisor packs, or object-alternates file.
- Live advertised-ref recheck: 1,629 refs; 0 missing; 7 changed since saved snapshot; 1 saved refs no longer advertised.
- LFS: 0 standard pointer blobs among 51,747 reachable blobs; 1,268 small blob contents scanned. `git lfs ls-files --all --long` exited 0 and returned 0 lines.
- Releases: saved **35 releases with 0 attached assets**; API page 1 contains 35 releases and page 2 is empty. Saved release IDs match page 1 and saved release tags match the 35 Git tags.

## Scope and limits

This audit covers the main REA repository, bundle restoration, LFS pointer history, and releases metadata. Submodules and dependency/runtime preservation are handled separately. GitHub discussions, issue and pull-request conversation contents, Actions logs/artifacts, and external dependencies are separate from Git history. A current mirror cannot include remote data that was already deleted or never advertised.

The detailed machine-readable results are in `metadata/backup-audit.json`. The isolated verification restore remains in `C:\Users\evanh\Documents\Codex\2026-10-12\go\work\rea-audit-restore`.
# REA operating guide

REA connects an agent or terminal to tools for investigating shipped software. Start with a specific feature and an exact local artifact, collect the smallest useful evidence, and follow unresolved details into code or runtime observations. The result is an explanation or reconstruction supported by evidence; original source recovery and complete behavioral equivalence require separate proof.

This guide accompanies the October 12, 2026 backup of [morluto/rea](https://github.com/morluto/rea). The saved source revision is `abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71`. Its package manifest says `6.4.0`, but repository main and the published npm package can contain different bytes and capabilities. The archived source documentation and the connected server's actual schemas govern the respective versions. [Local installation reference](source/docs/installation.md) Â· [Official installation reference](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/installation.md)

## Use the preserved local runtime

The backup includes an isolated `runtime` installation of the published `rea-agents` 6.4.0 package. From PowerShell, select this backup folder and use its launcher:

```powershell
Set-Location 'C:\Users\evanh\Documents\Codex\2026-10-12\go\outputs\rea-backup-2026-10-12'
.\rea.cmd --version
.\rea.cmd --help
```

The launcher uses the machine's `node` command and the preserved local package. It does not download a package for each call. All terminal examples below use this launcher from the backup folder; replace illustrative target paths and selectors with the actual target for your investigation.

The October 12 verification on this machine established the following results. The durable record is [runtime-verification.json](metadata/runtime-verification.json).

| Check | Verified result |
| --- | --- |
| Host runtime | Node v24.16.0, Windows x64 |
| Preserved CLI | Version 6.4.0 |
| Dependency installation | 144 packages installed with install scripts disabled |
| Offline recovery | Independent `npm ci --offline` using the preserved tarball and cache succeeded, also with install scripts disabled |
| Production stdio MCP | Healthy handshake, 170 of 170 tools, 6 of 6 prompts, matching package/catalog identity and successful `binary_session` request |
| Windows native artifact | Bundled artifact checksum verification passed |
| Static JavaScript sample | Two files parsed, zero parse failures, `findNotes` and `normalizeQuery` exports and `./search.js` import verified; application code was not executed |
| Follow-up workflows | Summary projection and application feature trace completed successfully |

These checks establish local package operation and the selected static workflows. Native providers were neither installed nor exercised. The native artifact check does not establish Ghidra or IDA engine operation. Live browser, Electron, Android device, firmware, .NET and commercial-engine behavior remain untested. No global software installation or agent registration was applied.

The saved follow-up request files provide directly repeatable local examples:

```powershell
.\rea.cmd inspect-analysis-view '.\metadata\javascript-summary-input.json' --json
.\rea.cmd trace-application-feature '.\metadata\javascript-trace-input.json' --json
```

They consume the saved sample Evidence rather than executing an application. The underlying request and result files are retained beside the verification record.

## Select the correct investigation

| Target or question | First useful operation | Additional requirement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Extracted JavaScript or Electron application, or ASAR | `analyze_javascript_application` | Compatible Node and npm; no disassembler |
| Native executable or library | `open_binary`, then focused searches or `analyze_function` | A compatible Hopper, Ghidra, or IDA provider |
| Ordinary .NET PE/CLI assembly | `inspect_managed_artifact`, then members | Built-in static parser; no .NET runtime or native engine |
| Android APK classes and methods | `inspect_android_package`, then focused Android tools | JADX headless MCP 0.7.1 and full JDK 17+ |
| APK, ZIP, IPA, MSIX, AppX or other package contents | Open the artifact and inspect its inventory | Format and host restrictions apply |
| Existing website | `list_browser_targets`, then `inspect_web_page` | Chrome-family browser with local CDP debugging endpoint |
| Existing Electron renderer | `list_electron_targets`, then `inspect_electron_page` | Existing local Electron CDP endpoint |
| Embedded Go build facts | `inspect_go_binary` | Built-in inspection; no Go installation |
| Android device acquisition | `inspect_adb_client`, then device/package discovery | Caller-supplied adb and connected device or emulator |

Choose by file contents and the question, rather than the extension alone. A .NET NativeAOT executable needs native analysis for method bodies; a single-file .NET host is not automatically unpacked by the managed parser. Normal architecture analysis of a complete source repository is better served by ordinary repository inspection. [Local routing instructions](source/.agents/skills/reverse-engineer-anything/SKILL.md) Â· [Official routing instructions](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/.agents/skills/reverse-engineer-anything/SKILL.md)

## Understand the version and Windows boundaries

Supported Node versions are 22.x from 22.19, 24.x from 24.11, and 26+. Node 23, 25 and prereleases are outside the supported range. The source's installation checkpoint identifies published npm 6.4.0 with commit `a56f7bb6` on October 11. New main-only functionality can therefore be absent from an installed 6.4.0 package. A source manifest with the same version number does not establish identical package contents.

Use the server's `tools/list` for actual schemas and `binary_session` for tool availability, provider binding and server identity. A tool may be advertised while unavailable on the current host or target. A successful registration check does not prove that this running chat loaded the server. [Local MCP contracts](source/docs/mcp-contracts.md) Â· [Official MCP contracts](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/mcp-contracts.md)

Windows native analysis has two practical routes:

- **Ghidra:** experimental Windows x64 support, Windows 10+, fixed local NTFS target and temporary storage, Ghidra 12.1.x and a full 64-bit JDK in Ghidra's declared range. The saved main boundary admits native x86 and x86-64 PE executables and DLLs. Other formats, architectures, GUI state and general parity are outside that boundary. Matching bundled Windows native controls are required. [Local Windows Ghidra guide](source/docs/windows-ghidra-p0.md) Â· [Official guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/windows-ghidra-p0.md)
- **IDA:** adapt an existing `mrexodia/ida-pro-mcp` installation. The attached legacy profile binds an already-open GUI target; modern headless mode owns a private database. Pass the original binary, not `.idb` or `.i64`. Headless REA and its supervisor must share the same host filesystem and native paths. IDA does not provide REA's `binary_overview`, and several dossier facets remain unavailable. [Local IDA guide](source/docs/ida-provider.md) Â· [Official guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/ida-provider.md)

Hopper supports macOS and Linux. Several auxiliary tools, including firmware extraction, historical-source import, JavaScript recovery, offline ELF diagnostics and process PTY capture, have Linux or macOS host requirements. Native Windows support for static JavaScript and .NET does not imply support for those separate workflows. [Local CLI reference](source/docs/cli.md) Â· [Official CLI reference](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/cli.md)

## Configure an agent only when needed

The backup does not itself register an MCP server. For future setup, first preview the exact Codex changes:

```powershell
.\rea.cmd setup --client codex --dry-run --json
.\rea.cmd doctor --client codex --json
```

These calls use the preserved release. Running `setup --dry-run` is a read-only plan. Review the proposed configuration path, matching skill destination, backups and provider settings before applying setup. The installed skill supplies instructions; it does not establish an MCP connection or install analysis engines. Restart or reconnect the agent after registration changes. [Local setup reference](source/docs/installation.md#review-setup-changes) Â· [Official setup reference](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/installation.md#review-setup-changes)

The production stdio test above launched and checked an isolated server; it did not attach REA to this chat. Registering a server remains a separate future setup action.

## Investigate one feature well

Define the observable action, input and output: for example, exporting a CSV, handling a search query, parsing a file, or calculating audio panning. Bind the exact artifact path and hash. Start with its overview or inventory, choose a literal string, route, API, channel or function as the entry point, then follow only the relationships needed to answer the question.

For each conclusion, retain the supporting Evidence ID, source location, authority, coverage and unresolved alternatives. Use runtime capture when the question requires execution evidence. Keep a short finding ledger and mark each original question answered, partial or unresolved. The MCP guided prompts include feature investigation, version comparison, reconstruction verification, crash tracing, unknown auditing and bounded process capture; they offer workflows and do not execute tools by themselves. [Local prompt guide](source/docs/mcp-prompts.md) Â· [Official prompt guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/mcp-prompts.md)

## Investigate JavaScript and Electron

Static application analysis reads a directory or ASAR without executing the app:

```powershell
.\rea.cmd analyze-javascript-application 'D:\targets\Example\resources\app.asar' --json > app-evidence.json
```

Keep accompanying `.asar.unpacked` files. Their absence leaves relevant native/resource bytes unavailable. By default, integrity mismatches fail; an explicit `--integrity-policy record-and-continue` records contradictory hashes and partial coverage while examining observed bytes. It does not make those bytes verified.

For large applications, use MCP `detail: "summary"` when advertised. This still performs analysis and retains the full record, returning a smaller view with `normalized_result.parent_evidence_id`. Source selection, when advertised, reduces actual parsing work: exact `source_paths` or repeated `--source-path` values select only the named files; dependencies are not added automatically. Include the manifest and source maps needed for your question. [Local artifact guide](source/docs/javascript-artifact-reconstruction.md) Â· [Official artifact guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/javascript-artifact-reconstruction.md)

Page module identities before selecting an exact node. In a single MCP connection, this `inspect_analysis_view` request projects a page from the retained analysis:

```json
{
  "source": { "kind": "retained-evidence", "evidence_id": "ACTUAL_PARENT_EVIDENCE_ID" },
  "view": { "kind": "page", "collection": "modules", "offset": 0, "limit": 16 }
}
```

Replace the placeholder with the returned ID, then use `view.kind: "item"`, `collection: "modules"` and `selector.node_id` to inspect a returned module. One path can represent multiple roles, so a node ID is a better selector than an unverified path. Use a feature trace only if a module view leaves an unanswered relationship question. [Local module recipes](source/.agents/skills/reverse-engineer-anything/references/javascript-applications.md) Â· [Official module recipes](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/.agents/skills/reverse-engineer-anything/references/javascript-applications.md)

For Electron, follow renderer API to preload `contextBridge`, literal IPC channel, main handler and output writer. A unique literal channel can support an inferred pairing; computed names and ambiguous handlers remain unresolved. In the official notes example, `notes.exportCsv()` crosses `notes:export` and reaches the main process CSV writer. That static path does not prove it executed. [Official Electron walkthrough](https://rea.tools/guides/javascript/)

`trace_application_feature` follows structural relationships; its exact direction vocabulary is `outgoing`, `incoming` or `both`. The local verification rejected `forward` and succeeded with `outgoing`. Do not substitute direction terms from native value-flow tools, whose schema is separate. `trace_javascript_semantics` follows supported static data and control relations. Narrow the direction where possible: a trace using `both` can include unrelated containment siblings. Compare two versions with authenticated graphs, preserving ambiguous mappings; added or removed requires complete opposite-side coverage. Minified names and module ordinals are not durable identities. [Local workflow guide](source/docs/javascript-application-workflows.md) Â· [Official workflow guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/javascript-application-workflows.md)

## Investigate native code

Choose a provider explicitly when more than one supports the target:

```powershell
.\rea.cmd providers --json
.\rea.cmd capabilities --json
.\rea.cmd doctor --provider ghidra --json
.\rea.cmd analyze 'D:\targets\program.exe' --provider ghidra --json
.\rea.cmd search 'D:\targets\program.exe' 'export' --provider ghidra --json
.\rea.cmd function 'D:\targets\program.exe' main --provider ghidra --json
.\rea.cmd instructions 'D:\targets\program.exe' 0x1000 --provider ghidra --json
.\rea.cmd xrefs 'D:\targets\program.exe' 0x1000 --provider ghidra --json
```

The last examples require a real returned function selector or address; `main` and `0x1000` are placeholders. A function dossier combines useful facets; pseudocode alone can omit important arithmetic, indirect calls or types. Check instructions, callers, references and original data bytes. In the official DX-Ball example, interpreting the instructions, caller argument and referenced doubles recovered a calculation absent from the first pseudocode. Its separate reconstruction checks covered 3,205 inputs and all 63 compiled function bytes. [Official native walkthrough](https://rea.tools/guides/native/)

For names beginning with a dash, use a named equals selector such as `--procedure=-[REAWidget delegate]`; otherwise a command-line parser may interpret the name as an option. An ambiguous symbol should be resolved to an exact returned address. CLI calls import, analyze and close independently; MCP follow-ups reuse the open native session. [Local native reference](source/docs/native-investigation.md) Â· [Official native reference](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/native-investigation.md)

## Inspect .NET and Android

For ordinary .NET assemblies, identify the deployment form before selecting members:

```powershell
.\rea.cmd inspect-managed-artifact 'D:\targets\Assembly.dll' --json
.\rea.cmd inspect-managed-members 'D:\targets\Assembly.dll' --type Example.Parser --json
```

Use an exact returned type name. Inspection reports metadata and CIL without loading the assembly. Tokens and addresses are specific to a build; native declarations identify candidate boundaries, not native semantics. Member selection leaves unselected types unknown, and reference inventories can still be large. The parser does not unpack single-file hosts or decode IL2CPP; NativeAOT bodies require the native route. [Local managed-code guide](source/docs/managed-code-analysis.md) Â· [Official guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/managed-code-analysis.md)

For Android APKs, first inspect package declarations, discover class names, inspect the class to choose an overload, then inspect that method and its incoming references:

```powershell
.\rea.cmd inspect-android-package 'D:\targets\Example.apk' --json
.\rea.cmd search-android-classes 'D:\targets\Example.apk' MainActivity --json
.\rea.cmd inspect-android-class 'D:\targets\Example.apk' example.MainActivity --json
.\rea.cmd inspect-android-method 'D:\targets\Example.apk' example.MainActivity onCreate --json
```

JADX-derived Java or smali is a representation, not original source. Manifest summaries should be checked against retained XML when material. APK signatures are not verified, references are static and overload indices are specific to the artifact/engine. Standalone split APK/AAB reconstruction, native-library analysis and runtime execution are outside this static family. Windows x64 requires the package's matching native controls. [Local Android guide](source/docs/android-analysis.md) Â· [Official guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/android-analysis.md)

Use adb tools to acquire a complete installed base/split APK set or inspect a selected device. Device paths become host paths only after acquisition. Pulls and captures preserve sizes and SHA-256; output destinations must be new. Installation, uninstallation, push, start and stop operations change the device and require that action to be within the requested scope. [Local adb guide](source/docs/adb-device-analysis.md) Â· [Official guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/adb-device-analysis.md)

## Observe a browser or Electron runtime

Passive CDP observation attaches to an existing target; it does not click, navigate, invoke IPC or evaluate page code. Supply a literal loopback HTTP endpoint with an explicit port, such as `http://127.0.0.1:9222`. Target IDs belong to that browser instance. For a user action, arm capture before performing it:

```powershell
.\rea.cmd list-browser-targets http://127.0.0.1:9222 --json
.\rea.cmd inspect-web-page http://127.0.0.1:9222 TARGET_ID --observation-ms 10000 --include-json-body-shapes --json
```

Prior network activity is unavailable. The default origin boundary and observation window limit conclusions. JSON shapes preserve structure without values; accessibility text and script sources require separate selections. Requested text, URLs, scripts and screenshots can still carry application information. REA does not generally anonymize every field. [Local browser guide](source/docs/browser-observation.md) Â· [Official guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/browser-observation.md)

For local Electron pages, use `list-electron-targets` and `inspect-electron-page` with their endpoint. Static and passive evidence can later be reconciled, preserving exact digest mappings and ambiguity. An observed bundle does not prove every enclosed module executed. Active Electron scenarios instead launch and drive the chosen executable under an owned lifecycle. [Local Electron guide](source/docs/electron-observation.md) Â· [Official guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/electron-observation.md)

Active browser scenarios also use a separate contract:

```powershell
.\rea.cmd capture-browser-scenario 'D:\analysis\browser-scenario.json' --json > browser-capture.json
```

Their JSON request specifies browser launch/connect selection, start URL and declared actions. Request DOM, accessibility, screenshots or events that answer the question, and wait for an application-specific readiness marker. Default URL-only capture cannot establish that rendered results appeared. A complete capture covers selected sections only. Active runtime ownership does not make the application a security sandbox. [Local scenario contract](source/docs/browser-scenario-contract.md) Â· [Official contract](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/browser-scenario-contract.md)

## Preserve evidence and interpret claims

Read `normalized_result` for findings and retain `evidence_id` for references. Distinguish directly observed artifact bytes/metadata, static inferences and runtime observations. Unsupported, missing, truncated, unselected and unavailable fields remain unknown. An empty unsupported facet is not evidence of absence. A reconstruction verification pass covers its finite declared specification; it does not prove global equivalence. [Local evidence instructions](source/.agents/skills/reverse-engineer-anything/references/evidence-workflows.md) Â· [Official evidence instructions](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/.agents/skills/reverse-engineer-anything/references/evidence-workflows.md)

There are three different things to save:

| Saved object | What it preserves | Reuse boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Complete CLI JSON record | One operation's Evidence | Separate CLI workflows consume the complete saved record |
| Exported Evidence bundle | Session records, manifests and unknown history | Validate/import on a later connection |
| Analysis snapshot | Eligible exact cached results and target-scoped history | Same target bytes, operation, parameters, provider and settings |

In MCP, export before closing:

```json
{"name":"export_evidence_bundle","arguments":{"path":"D:\\analysis\\session-evidence.json"}}
{"name":"close_binary","arguments":{}}
```

These are separate calls in the shown order. Existing export destinations require explicit `overwrite: true`. Opening a different target preserves retained records; `close_binary` clears the ledger even if no binary is active. Another connection or standalone CLI process cannot resolve a previous connection's retained ID. Import the bundle or supply full inline Evidence. Saving a snapshot is distinct from saving a provider database. After a metadata write has been dispatched, close and reopen before importing or saving another immutable snapshot. [Local lifecycle reference](source/docs/mcp-contracts.md) Â· [Local snapshot reference](source/docs/cli.md#save-and-reuse-analysis-snapshots)

## Recover from practical problems

| Symptom | Useful next step |
| --- | --- |
| Tools absent after setup | Check the selected client registration and restart/reconnect; file readiness is not live connection proof |
| Overall doctor report unhealthy | Scope it to the required client/provider; unrelated optional checks may fail |
| Multiple candidate providers | Choose the returned provider ID explicitly |
| Provider unavailable | Diagnose that provider, preserving its original error reason |
| Large JSON result or truncated preview | Inspect a retained summary/module/page, or export the bundle; do not repeat completed analysis merely to redisplay it |
| MCP transport resource constraint | Use its exact reported Evidence reference or continuation; reduce view size or export |
| Client timeout during Ghidra import | Inspect `analysis_activity`; the provider may still be analyzing |
| Native startup deadline too short | Consider `REA_GHIDRA_STARTUP_TIMEOUT_MS` or `REA_HOPPER_STARTUP_TIMEOUT_MS`; the client's own deadline is separate |
| JavaScript parsing resource limit | Read partial coverage and the affected source before changing the advertised heap/deadline settings |
| Cleanup incomplete | Preserve reported workspace and ownership diagnostics; resolve only the identified resources |

Use `--json` when saving results. Exit status zero can still contain partial evidence and unresolved questions, and a cancelled setup also exits zero. Parse operation status and coverage rather than equating a process exit with a complete answer. Main-only options must be checked against the exact runtime before use. [Local readiness reference](source/docs/installation.md#check-readiness-for-your-task) Â· [Local CLI status reference](source/docs/cli.md#output-and-exit-status) Â· [Official readiness reference](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71/docs/installation.md#check-readiness-for-your-task)

## Ask an agent for a focused result

> Use REA to investigate how this exact local app performs [feature]. Start with the appropriate artifact workflow. Keep the original files intact, identify the target digest, and show the evidence and source locations for each conclusion. Separate static inference from observed execution. Follow specific unresolved details, preserve the session evidence, and list any limits that affect the answer. If a reconstruction is requested, test the declared inputs and outputs against the original behavior.

For a new investigation, add the absolute target path, feature trigger, known inputs, expected output and desired deliverable. For runtime work, also specify the target endpoint or executable and intended actions. REA analyzes locally, but returned tool content is shared with the agent's model provider under that provider's data policy. [Official repository overview](https://github.com/morluto/rea) Â· [Local overview](source/README.md)
# REA local backup and recovery

This folder preserves [Reverse Engineer Anything](https://github.com/morluto/rea) as a local October 12, 2026 snapshot. It contains the source and documentation, Git history, its five submodule repositories, and an isolated working copy of the published `rea-agents` 6.4.0 package. The main repository can be recovered from the bundle without contacting GitHub.

The saved main revision is `abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71`. The upstream repository continued changing during preservation; this folder retains the verified snapshot. Its Git mirror contains 75 branches, 35 tags, 1,520 pull-request refs and 5,830 reachable commits. Git history contains code and commits; discussion threads and other GitHub services are separate data.

## Run the preserved tool

Open PowerShell in this folder:

```powershell
.\rea.cmd --version
.\rea.cmd --help
.\rea.cmd analyze-javascript-application 'D:\targets\extracted-app' --json
```

The local launcher uses the preserved runtime and this machine's existing Node installation. It needs a supported Node version: 22.x from 22.19, 24.x from 24.11, or 26+. Testing here used Node 24.16.0 on Windows x64. For regular investigation recipes and provider requirements, read [REA Usage Guide](REA-Usage-Guide.md).

The CLI, static JavaScript analysis, saved-evidence summary and feature tracing passed local checks. The production MCP server passed its identity, 170-tool inventory, six-prompt inventory and request-flow checks. Native engine, browser and device analysis require their respective external tools; the Windows native artifact was checksum-verified, without exercising Ghidra or IDA. No global agent registration was applied.

## What each copy preserves

| Item | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `rea.git/` | Bare mirror of all advertised main repository refs and reachable history |
| `rea-complete.bundle` | Standalone Git history file for offline recovery |
| `source/` | Readable source, documentation, licenses and pinned submodule working copies |
| `submodule-mirrors/` and `submodule-bundles/` | Separate submodule history and offline recovery files |
| `packages/rea-agents-6.4.0.tgz` | Original published package, verified against the npm SHA-512 integrity value |
| `runtime/` | Local installed runtime and locked dependencies, isolated from global installations |
| `npm-cache/` | Cached package content and metadata used for a successful offline reinstall |
| `metadata/` | Repository, release and package metadata, audit results and runtime evidence |
| `REA-Usage-Guide.md` | Investigation workflows, commands, evidence rules and Windows constraints |
| `SUBMODULE-RESTORE.md` | Submodule and large-file recovery instructions, including Windows checkout limits |
| `checksums.json` | File sizes and SHA-256 digests for integrity checking |

Keep the whole folder together. Git bundles preserve Git objects but do not embed Git LFS payloads. The submodule recovery guide identifies the separately preserved large-file data. Windows cannot check out every upstream test-fixture filename; the affected submodule uses a documented sparse checkout, while its original objects remain preserved for recovery on a compatible system.

Keep this dated backup intact when updating REA. Use a separate working copy for upgrades and save investigation outputs outside the preserved source and runtime.

## Verify the backup

```powershell
.\Verify-Backup.ps1
```

This reads the stored file digests and checks the main Git mirror. It reports missing or changed files. The independently tested main Git restoration is documented in [Backup Audit](BACKUP-AUDIT.md), and runtime results are saved in `metadata/runtime-verification.json`.

## Restore the source without GitHub

Choose a new destination that does not already exist:

```powershell
.\Restore-Repository.ps1 -Destination 'D:\REA-Restored'
```

The script restores the Git mirror and main source revision, then checks repository integrity. Follow [Submodule Restore](SUBMODULE-RESTORE.md) to restore the pinned submodule data. The script never replaces an existing destination.

To rebuild the runtime dependencies from the saved package and cache, use the existing `runtime` directory or a copy with the same `packages`, `runtime` and `npm-cache` layout:

```powershell
npm.cmd ci --prefix .\runtime --offline --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund --cache .\npm-cache
.\rea.cmd --version
```

This exact offline installation method was tested in a separate folder. It replaces `runtime/node_modules` in the selected copy, using its lockfile. The backup already contains a working installation, so reinstalling is a recovery option. Dependency installation scripts were disabled in both the saved runtime and the tested restore.

## Coverage

The snapshot preserves all Git refs advertised when it was cloned and their reachable history. It also saves metadata for all 35 releases; none had attached release assets. No Git LFS pointer was found in the main repository's reachable history. Submodule large files are covered separately.

GitHub issues and pull-request conversations, Discussions, Actions logs and artifacts, and unadvertised or previously deleted data are outside this backup. External native analysis engines, their licenses, browser installations, Java, Android tooling and source-build development dependencies are also outside the preserved runtime. The copy is local to this disk; copying the whole folder to another device would add protection against disk failure.

For a later investigation, point the agent to `REA-Usage-Guide.md` and the exact target artifact. The matching published workflow skill is also preserved under `runtime/node_modules/rea-agents/skills/`; the source-tree skill may describe newer capabilities than the published package.
[CmdletBinding()]
param([Parameter(Mandatory = $true)][string]$Destination)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
$backupRoot = $PSScriptRoot
$restoreRoot = [System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath($Destination)
if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $restoreRoot) {
    throw "Restore destination already exists. Choose a new directory: $restoreRoot"
}
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $restoreRoot | Out-Null
& git clone --mirror (Join-Path $backupRoot 'rea-complete.bundle') (Join-Path $restoreRoot 'rea.git')
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Mirror restoration failed.' }
& git clone --no-hardlinks (Join-Path $restoreRoot 'rea.git') (Join-Path $restoreRoot 'source')
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Source restoration failed.' }
& git -C (Join-Path $restoreRoot 'source') checkout --detach 'abc8c6dadf4f11b67de1cc2ee96bb55047416d71'
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Saved revision checkout failed.' }
$restoreGitDir = Join-Path $restoreRoot 'rea.git'
& git "--git-dir=$restoreGitDir" fsck --full
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Restored repository integrity verification failed.' }
Write-Output "REA main repository restored and verified at $restoreRoot"
Write-Output 'Restore its five submodules and any LFS data using SUBMODULE-RESTORE.md in the backup.'
# Restore the preserved REA submodules offline

The five repositories in `submodule-mirrors/` preserve every upstream Git ref advertised at capture and the history reachable from those refs. `submodule-bundles/*.bundle` contain the same Git history. Exact REA gitlink commits, upstream URLs, counts, checksums, licenses, and verification results are recorded in `metadata/submodules.json`.

Git LFS data is separate from Git bundles. **Retain `submodule-mirrors/unblob.git/lfs/objects/`**: it contains 1,509 payloads totaling 1,361,461,226 bytes, with every SHA-256 and size verified. No advertised-history LFS object was missing. A bundle alone cannot recover these payloads.

The saved `source/` already has all five submodule HEADs at the required commits. No submodule contains a nested gitlink at its pinned revision. All Git mirror checks and all bundle verifications passed.

## Native Windows limitation

unblob's archive fixtures contain 11 paths Windows cannot represent faithfully (control characters, a question mark, a double quote, and trailing spaces or dots). Its source checkout omits precisely those paths through sparse checkout; the exact files and LFS content remain in the mirrors. See `metadata/submodule-verification-unblob-sparse-excluded.txt` and `metadata/submodule-verification-unblob-sparse-patterns.txt`.

Windows Git also represents symlinks as files containing their exact target text (`core.symlinks=false`). unblob has a local `info/attributes` override that disables the LFS filter only for its symlink paths, preventing Git LFS from interpreting these link-target files as payloads. Executable-bit and symlink semantics require a compatible filesystem for faithful restoration. A Linux checkout can restore all fixture paths and actual symlinks.

## Restore into a fresh main checkout on Windows

First restore the main REA repository using the main backup instructions. Point `$reaWork` below to that **fresh checkout**, which must have empty submodule directories. These commands use only local mirrors and metadata; they do not contact GitHub, install software, or run REA.

```powershell
$backupRoot = 'C:\Users\evanh\Documents\Codex\2026-10-12\go\outputs\rea-backup-2026-10-12'
$reaWork = 'C:\path\to\fresh-restored-rea'
$manifest = Get-Content -LiteralPath "$backupRoot\metadata\submodules.json" -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json
$env:GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE = '1'

foreach ($entry in $manifest.repositories) {
    $mirrorPath = Join-Path $backupRoot $entry.mirror
    $modulePath = Join-Path $reaWork $entry.path
    git -C $reaWork config "submodule.$($entry.path).url" $mirrorPath
    git -c core.autocrlf=false -c core.longpaths=true clone --no-checkout --no-hardlinks $mirrorPath $modulePath
    if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "Clone failed: $($entry.name)" }
    git -C $modulePath config core.autocrlf false
    git -C $modulePath config core.longpaths true

    if ($entry.name -eq 'unblob') {
        $moduleGitDir = git -C $modulePath rev-parse --absolute-git-dir
        New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path "$moduleGitDir\info" -Force | Out-Null
        Copy-Item -LiteralPath "$backupRoot\metadata\submodule-verification-unblob-sparse-patterns.txt" -Destination "$moduleGitDir\info\sparse-checkout"
        Copy-Item -LiteralPath "$backupRoot\metadata\submodule-verification-unblob-symlink-local-attributes.txt" -Destination "$moduleGitDir\info\attributes"
        git -C $modulePath config core.sparseCheckout true
        git -C $modulePath config core.sparseCheckoutCone false
        git -C $modulePath config lfs.storage "$backupRoot/submodule-mirrors/unblob.git/lfs"
        # Only for this sparse checkout invocation; excluded paths are not written.
        git -c core.protectNTFS=false -C $modulePath checkout --detach $entry.pinnedCommit
    } else {
        git -C $modulePath checkout --detach $entry.pinnedCommit
    }
    if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "Checkout failed: $($entry.name)" }
}
git -C $reaWork submodule status
```

The sparse index still records the exact original names; no names are rewritten. Do not disable sparse checkout on native Windows or run `git submodule sync` before restoring the local URL overrides, because sync copies GitHub URLs back from `.gitmodules`.

If Git LFS is available, optionally materialize the Windows-compatible fixture files from the local LFS object store. The precomputed path list excludes the 11 incompatible paths. Running unrestricted `git lfs checkout` on Windows attempts those paths despite sparse checkout, so use this list:

```powershell
$unblobWork = Join-Path $reaWork 'third_party/unblob'
$safePaths = @(Get-Content -LiteralPath "$backupRoot\metadata\submodule-verification-unblob-lfs-windows-checkout-paths.json" -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json)
for ($offset = 0; $offset -lt $safePaths.Count; $offset += 20) {
    $last = [Math]::Min($offset + 19, $safePaths.Count - 1)
    $pathBatch = @($safePaths[$offset..$last])
    git -C $unblobWork lfs checkout @pathBatch
    if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Local LFS checkout failed' }
}
git -C $unblobWork status --porcelain
```

These files are already materialized in the preserved source checkout wherever Windows supports their original names. The retained Git LFS object store is the authoritative recovery copy, including all historical payloads.

## Recovery when only bundles and LFS objects remain

Recreate each mirror from its bundle, then run the local restoration above. Copy the retained LFS object directory into the recreated unblob mirror's `lfs/objects/` before materializing files.

```powershell
git clone --mirror 'C:\saved\submodule-bundles\unblob.bundle' 'C:\recovered\submodule-mirrors\unblob.git'
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path 'C:\recovered\submodule-mirrors\unblob.git\lfs' -Force | Out-Null
Copy-Item -LiteralPath 'C:\saved\submodule-mirrors\unblob.git\lfs\objects' -Destination 'C:\recovered\submodule-mirrors\unblob.git\lfs\objects' -Recurse
git -C 'C:\recovered\submodule-mirrors\unblob.git' fsck --full --strict
```

Repeat the mirror clone for `jadx-headless-mcp`, `binwalk`, `ghidra-nativeaot`, and `wakaru`; they have no advertised Git LFS payloads. Set `$backupRoot` to the recovered folder, preserving the same metadata and directory layout.

## Full checkout on Linux or another compatible filesystem

Clone each mirror locally without checkout, set the mirror's local LFS storage location for unblob, and check out the pinned commit from `submodules.json`. Omit the Windows sparse and `info/attributes` overrides, use actual symlinks, and leave `GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE=1` while checking out. Run `git lfs checkout` afterward to read payloads from the local store. This route restores the excluded fixture paths too; it has not been executed on this Windows machine.

The Git and LFS captures do not include inaccessible or unadvertised upstream objects, GitHub discussions, external package registries, or each dependency's release attachments. Upstream license and notice files remain preserved in every checkout and in full history.
[CmdletBinding()]
param()

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
$backupRoot = [System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath($PSScriptRoot)
$prefix = $backupRoot.TrimEnd([char[]]@('\', '/')) + [System.IO.Path]::DirectorySeparatorChar
$inventoryPath = Join-Path $backupRoot 'checksums.json'
$expectedInventoryHash = ((Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath (Join-Path $backupRoot 'checksums.sha256')).Trim() -split '\s+')[0]
$actualInventoryHash = (Get-FileHash -LiteralPath $inventoryPath -Algorithm SHA256).Hash.ToLowerInvariant()
if ($actualInventoryHash -ne $expectedInventoryHash) { throw 'Checksum inventory integrity check failed.' }
$inventory = Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath $inventoryPath | ConvertFrom-Json
$failures = [System.Collections.Generic.List[string]]::new()
$checked = 0
foreach ($entry in $inventory.files) {
    $filePath = [System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath((Join-Path $backupRoot $entry.path))
    if (-not $filePath.StartsWith($prefix, [System.StringComparison]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)) {
        throw "Inventory path escapes the backup: $($entry.path)"
    }
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $filePath -PathType Leaf)) {
        $failures.Add("Missing: $($entry.path)")
        continue
    }
    $actual = (Get-FileHash -LiteralPath $filePath -Algorithm SHA256).Hash.ToLowerInvariant()
    if ($actual -ne $entry.sha256) { $failures.Add("Changed: $($entry.path)") }
    $checked++
    if ($checked % 1000 -eq 0) { Write-Progress -Activity 'Verifying preserved REA files' -Status "$checked files checked" }
}
Write-Progress -Activity 'Verifying preserved REA files' -Completed
if ($failures.Count -gt 0) {
    $failures | Write-Output
    throw "$($failures.Count) file integrity checks failed."
}
$mirrorGitDir = Join-Path $backupRoot 'rea.git'
& git "--git-dir=$mirrorGitDir" fsck --full
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Main Git mirror integrity check failed.' }
Write-Output "Verified $checked files and the main Git mirror."
Write-Output 'The checksum inventory excludes itself and its standalone checksum text file.'
